Cut the CPU a worker adds, and stop the version lying

Two findings, both measured.

**One worker was opening four threads.** On a real 1h52m remux, the bundled ffsubsync's numeric
libraries peak at 4 threads per process; pinned via OMP/OPENBLAS/MKL/NUMEXPR/VECLIB they peak at
1 thread while doing the same work at the same ~100% of a core. Worker count and thread count
multiply, so four workers on a four-core box were demanding up to sixteen threads - a load
average above the core count, with no user-visible cause. ffsubsync is now launched with those
variables set, and children inherit them. The worker setting remains the only thing that decides
parallelism; nothing else changes per worker.

**The version was lying.** The catalog said 1.1.0.30 while the assembly still said 1.1.0.19
(AssemblyVersion/FileVersion had not moved since that release), so the version shown in the
interface - read from the assembly - reported the wrong build. Both now expand from <Version>, so
a release cannot ship a mismatch: verified, the built DLL contains 1.1.0.31 and nothing else.

Harness: 117 checks, ALL PASS.

Version 1.1.0.31.
